{
  "abstract": "Alpöge and Furman proved unconditionally that at least 2/3 of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function are simple and on the critical line. Their certificate uses the trace and Hilbert–Schmidt norm of a Gram matrix built from the zeros through a Gabor frame of bandwidth L = log(T/2π). An isolated off-line pair at depth β − 1/2 = ε adds about e^{2εL}/(2ε²L²) to that norm, which suggests that these data might bound the number of zeros far from the line. We show that they cannot. Theorem 1: a lattice of reflected pairs with spacing at most the frame step 2π/L has exactly the Gram matrix of on-line double zeros at the same ordinates, at every depth, for every window supported in an interval of length L. So does every pair sum of bandwidth at most one. The proof is the Poisson step of Alpöge–Furman's Lemma 2.1, with zeros and frame exchanged. Proposition 2: for the indicator window, a chain of m pairs at depth c = εL costs (4 sinh² c/π²)(log m + C(c)) + O(1/m) more than the doubles, with C explicit. Corollary 3: in a model of the certificate (indicator window, untruncated frame), the Alpöge–Furman extremal configuration with its doubles replaced by such chains keeps trace N and Hilbert–Schmidt norm (4/3 + o(1))N while a third of its zeros lie at depth ε, for every fixed ε in (0, 1/2). These data therefore give no zero-density estimate beyond N(σ) ≤ (1/6 + o(1))N for any σ < 1. Aryan observed that going beyond 2/3 requires the negative spectral mass of off-line pairs not to cancel completely; in the model it cancels completely at every depth. Numerically optimised chains cost 50 times less at c = 4 and 10⁴ times less at c = 8.",
